  something like this  even you know the president's press  secretary was asked about shakiri  richardson and they acknowledge she's a  phenomenal athlete  she has an inspiring story but obviously  she will not be competing and she's  one of the world's the world's fastest  woman and jamil it's not as you know a  performance-enhancing drug either  no unless you have a bag of doritos at  the finish line  but um what i what i would say  is that you have to look at why is this  rule in place and it seems to be more of  a perception issue  than actually going with what is the  science i mean the fact is in 40  countries there is some form  of legalized marijuana we know where the  attitudes are moving in the states  and so much like the professional  leagues in america the nfl the nba major  league baseball  there's a reason they've all relaxed  their standards it has been used as pain  management and talking about marijuana  it is used in terms of coping mentally  and there are many athletes that came  forward  after what happened with shakiri  richardson to say that it helps them  with their anxiety  and i think we would much rather this  alternative  than i don't know alcohol or any other  sort of form of  any other dangerous substance i mean  considering all this young lady has been  through  we need to be happy the only thing she  did was smoke weed yeah just because  it's a rule doesn't mean it's right and  maybe we all need to look at  look at the rules because many of them  seem to be very outdated  and seem to be hurting black women in  particular  uh yeah i mean i i think when we assess  generally what has been our drug policy  in this country we know what  disproportionately affects  i was thinking about that swim cap rule  i wasn't even aware of that  yeah can we talk about the fallout  that's going on over at espn right now a  year after the audio  surfaced of rachel nichols talking about  maria taylor can you can you can you  briefly tell us what that's about and  your thoughts on it you worked at espn  you know the shop very well  yeah yeah i worked there for 12 years i  know both of these women  um and they're both very good we should  say they're both very good at what they  do  yeah yes they're vote they're both very  good both um  very admired and uh professionally by a  lot of people in this business and  essentially  in a private conversation that was um  you know that a lot of people at espn  heard because  uh it was on a company server uh you  know rachel  said some very disparaging things about  you know maria and  there was an assignment that was  promised to her contractually  and that assignment was going to go to  maria and kind of all hell is broken  loose and  listen gail you've been in tv for a long  time we know that  what happens behind the scenes it's a  very competitive business  sometimes it does not bring out the best  in people and  i think here what needs to be addressed  is culturally  how these things happen like what is  allowed to persist in certain  workplaces and i think i'm disappointed  in the fact that  you know maria has this very high  profile job it's great to see a black  woman have a job like that  it just does not seem like the company's  support was there  the fact that they would promise this  assignment to rachel nichols  i mean this is maria's job like why  would you even do that to begin with  because the fine the person who hosts  nba countdown  usually does the finals so to me that's  some kind of indication  that the support for maria hasn't been  there from the beginning and that's a  problem  i took the comments more disparaging  against espn than i did to maria  well but here's the other thing that  this touches on i mean i think  black women especially black people in  the workplace  we get a little tired of somebody  insinuating that the reason that we have  a job  is because we're black yes that gets  very tiresome  and i i know that um you know especially  in this business especially in sports  where you don't see a lot of black women  in the role  that maria is in she hears that a  hundred times yes  and so to hear that from a colleague  another woman i can imagine that that  would be very harmful and hurtful for  her and it is tiresome you're right  jamal have you spoke to either of the  women  uh i have not i mean i listen uh marie  and i have a good relationship  and what i don't want to do is be one of  these people that's hitting her up  when it's a whole lot of mess out there  i'll wait till things quiet down and  i'll reach out to her  but some of the other women at the  company's company some of the other  black women  i have reached out to them because i  know when you see a story like that  um you begin to question your colleagues  the environment  i understand i want to read what espn  said in a statement it says a diverse  group of executives thoroughly and  fairly considered all the facts related  to the incident  and then addressed the situation  appropriately  again you work there you know the ins  and outs and i think it's unfortunate  and complicated  situation but a conversation that needs  to be had jamelle hill  we thank you for joining us complicated  is the word  there and unfortunate yeah thank you  angel tiresome  thank you right now let's take a look at  your

